WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.460
Object-relational mappers, or ORMs, help Python developers map application
objects to database records and query APIs.

2
00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:17.961
They are common in Django applications, SQLAlchemy-style data layers,
Flask services, FastAPI services, scripts, internal tools, background
jobs, and data pipelines.

3
00:00:17.961 --> 00:00:28.534
An ORM can make database access more expressive and maintainable, but it
is still a security boundary because it decides what data is read,
changed, serialized, and exposed.

4
00:00:28.534 --> 00:00:38.167
Safe ORM usage means understanding the difference between models, query
APIs, raw SQL, migrations, validation, authorization, and database
permissions.

5
00:00:38.167 --> 00:00:42.222
A model describes an application-facing representation of data.

6
00:00:42.222 --> 00:00:44.685
A query API builds database operations.

7
00:00:44.685 --> 00:00:47.075
Raw SQL may bypass safer abstractions.

8
00:00:47.075 --> 00:00:49.465
Migrations change structure and data.

9
00:00:49.465 --> 00:00:51.058
Validation checks input.

10
00:00:51.058 --> 00:00:54.389
Authorization decides who may access which resource.

11
00:00:54.389 --> 00:00:58.372
Database permissions limit what the application account can do.

12
00:00:58.372 --> 00:00:59.966
Each layer has its own job.

13
00:00:59.966 --> 00:01:07.932
ORMs reduce some risk, especially when developers use safe query APIs and
parameter binding instead of assembling query strings.

14
00:01:07.932 --> 00:01:11.553
But they do not automatically make database access secure.

15
00:01:11.553 --> 00:01:25.314
Developers can bypass safe paths, expose sensitive fields through generic
serializers, forget tenant filters, accept too many writable fields, build
dynamic query fragments unsafely, or run raw SQL without enough review.

16
00:01:25.314 --> 00:01:27.341
The ORM is a tool, not a guarantee.

17
00:01:27.341 --> 00:01:36.105
Safe ORM usage must cover query construction, model design, access
control, serialization, transactions, migrations, logging, and review.

18
00:01:36.105 --> 00:01:41.898
Developers should know when they are using normal safe ORM paths and when
they are leaving them.

19
00:01:41.898 --> 00:01:49.720
The goal is predictable, testable data access that preserves business
rules and security boundaries as the application grows.
